1 CONC ></p></p><p><p>Daniel Friel's  name continues to exist in Fanad in  200
1 CONC 0 in the reference to the farm and house in Ballymichael as "Donie's". H
1 CONC e is recorded as "Daniel Freil" in the 1901 Census, He died on 31 Dec 19
1 CONC 03 in Ballymichael, based on his death record as reviewed in the registr
1 CONC ars office in Letterkenny in Aug 2005. His death was notified by Art, hi
1 CONC s son. He gives his age as 65 in the 1901 Census, hence the assumption r
1 CONC e his date of birth. His age is also noted as 68 on his death record, wh
1 CONC ich gives chronic diphteria as the cause of death. The 1901 Census note
1 CONC s him as a farmer, living in Ballymichael, states that he can read and w
1 CONC rite, and speaks both Irish and English. The locations given for his bir
1 CONC th is on the assumption that he was born in the location (Ballymichael
1 CONC ) in which his father (George Seoirse Friel) was recorded as living in 1
1 CONC 834 - as "George Freel" -  in the Tithes Applotments, and that he also d
1 CONC ied at this location.</p><p><p>Big Paddy Callaghan relates how Donie an
1 CONC d his brother Michael at one stage agreed to split the farm between them
1 CONC . This was contrary to the wishes of the landlord, Lord Leitrim who evic
1 CONC ted them both. Donie went to stay with relatives in Rosnakill ( and som
1 CONC e of the family are noted as being born in Rosnakill in the late 1870s )
1 CONC , but was subsequently allowed to return to the farm. Paddy also suggest
1 CONC ed that Donie may have spent some time in Lifford Gaol as a result o
1 CONC f a clash with Lord Leitrim - he may have been convicted of stealing tur
1 CONC f.  Eileen Carragher tells that Donie undertook the drainage of the bi
1 CONC g field on the left of the lane from the road to Donie's house in Ballym
1 CONC ichael. His brother, Michael went to Cashel near Portsalon, when his des
1 CONC cendants still live. The date of his marriage was obtained from the LD
1 CONC S site.</p><p><p>t</p></p><p></p>
