0 @N4486@ NOTE Census records were collected according to a national program and w
1 CONC ere taken in the same manner throughout Scotland. The records were subse
1 CONC quently preserved centrally and survive as a robust record of the ninete
1 CONC enth century from 1841 to 1901. This census was collected and organize
1 CONC d around enumeration districts and sub-districts. Enumeration district
1 CONC s correspond to parishes -- with some exceptions. The place of residenc
1 CONC e is often broken down into the name of street, house name or number, co
1 CONC unty, and parish.
0 @N4487@ NOTE Begun in 1935 by the Social Security Act signed into law by FDR, mo
1 CONC re than thirty million Americans were registered for the economic securi
1 CONC ty sanctions by 1937. From 1937 to 1940, payments were made in one-lum
1 CONC p sums amounts with the first amount being seventeen cents. Following am
1 CONC endments in 1939, the payments turned into monthly benefits and increase
1 CONC d. Following further amendments in 1950, cost-of-living increases were a
1 CONC warded to those who were receiving benefits. From 1950 to the present, b
1 CONC enefits have increased yearly in response to inflation concerning the co
1 CONC sts of living.
0 @N4488@ NOTE Death certificates represent one of the key primary sources for fam
1 CONC ily information, typically being issued within days of a death and havin
1 CONC g many details about a persons' life. Frequently, they contain age, birt
1 CONC hplace, parents' names and birthplaces and the cause of death.
0 @N4489@ NOTE Each record includes first and last name, date of birth, date of de
1 CONC ath and district of registration. Records prior to 1984 include only th
1 CONC e quarter of the year of death while since 1984 the month of death was r
1 CONC ecorded.
0 @N4490@ NOTE The 1870 Census was the first census to provide detailed informatio
1 CONC n on the black population, only years after the culmination of the Civi
1 CONC l War when slaves were granted freedom. The 1870 Census' population esti
1 CONC mate is controversial, as many believed it underestimated the true popul
1 CONC ation numbers, especially in New York and Pennsylvania.Federal census ta
1 CONC kers were asked to record information about every person who was in eac
1 CONC h household on the census day. A census taker might have visited a hous
1 CONC e on a later date, but the information he collected was supposed to be a
1 CONC bout the people who were in the house on the census day. The basic censu
1 CONC s enumeration unit was the county. Each county was divided into enumerat
1 CONC ion districts, one for each enumerator. The completed forms were sent t
1 CONC o the Commerce Department's Census Office in Washington, D.C.Federal cen
1 CONC suses are usually reliable, depending on the knowledge of the informan
1 CONC t and the care of the census enumerator. Information may have been give
1 CONC n to a census taker by any member of the family or by a neighbor. Some i
1 CONC nformation may have been incorrect or deliberately falsified.
0 @N4491@ NOTE <A href="http://www.billiongraves.com/" target="_blank">www.billion
1 CONC graves.com
0 @N4492@ NOTE Starting in 1936 the Social Security Administration started to main
1 CONC tain records of each individual who applied for a Social Security Number
1 CONC . The earliest form of these records were known as the “Master Files o
1 CONC f the Social Security (SSN) Holders and SSN Applications”. This was mor
1 CONC e commonly known simply as the “Enumeration System”. In the 1970s legac
1 CONC y records from this system were migrated and new records were maintaine
1 CONC d electronically in the Numerical Identification System (NUMIDENT). Thi
1 CONC s collection contains records of individuals with a verified death betwe
1 CONC en 1936 and 2007 or who would have been over 110 years old by December 3
1 CONC 1, 2007. There are three types of entries in NUMIDENT: applications (SS-
1 CONC 5), claims, and death entries. The records of applications and claims ar
1 CONC e presented here in this collection. The death entries are available o
1 CONC n MyHeritage as the U.S. Social Security Death Index (SSDI). The applica
1 CONC tion (SS-5) records contain information extracted from the SS-5 form “Ap
1 CONC plication for a Social Security Card” or “Application for Social Securit
1 CONC y Account Number.” Information in the NUMIDENT application entries inclu
1 CONC de applicant’s full name, father’s name, mother’s maiden name, sex, race
1 CONC /ethnic description, place of birth, and other information about the app
1 CONC lication and subsequent changes to the applicant’s record - such as nam
1 CONC e changes especially common (and even required) when women marry. For ab
1 CONC out 43 percent of social security numbers, there are multiple applicatio
1 CONC n records. The application records preserved by the Social Security Admi
1 CONC nistration do not include records of all social security applications be
1 CONC tween 1936 and 2007. Information of applications prior to 1973 may be in
1 CONC complete. There may not be a record for an individual in both the applic
1 CONC ation records and the death entries and there are nearly 6 million socia
1 CONC l security numbers in the application records that do not appear in th
1 CONC e death entries. And conversely, there are records in the death entrie
1 CONC s that have no corresponding extant record in the application entries. T
1 CONC he claim records include information on the type of claim, the claimant
1 CONC ’s full name, date of birth, gender, and for about half of the claim rec
1 CONC ords the US state or country of birth. For a small number of social secu
1 CONC rity numbers there are multiple claim records. Records in this collectio
1 CONC n may have place names that were abbreviated or personal names that wer
1 CONC e truncated in the data supplied by the Social Security Administration
1 CONC . MyHeritage has corrected and expanded many of these when possible bu
1 CONC t other values remain abbreviated or truncated.
